piece of work sound unfinished — they cannot tell whether "committed to the repo"
means they can use it. Say what changed and what they can do with it.

**Address Peter as "Sir Peter"** in anything written for the client side —
documents, headings and messages alike (Ali, 2026-07-31). "Waiting on Peter" as
a section heading reads like an internal ticket about him; "Waiting on Sir
Peter" is how the team refers to him.

**Everything sent to the client is written in ENGLISH.** Ali and I talk in Roman
Urdu; that is the internal channel and it stops at him. On 2026-08-08 I drafted a
message to Sir Peter in Roman Urdu and Ali pulled me up on it — Peter does not
read it. Two languages in one reply is the trap: the explanation to Ali is Roman
Urdu, the block he forwards is English, every time.

**How to apply:**
- Messages stay short and plain — Ali's earlier rule still stands: *"jo ek pagal
  ko bhi samjh ajaye"*. One idea per line.
- Translate, do not omit: "the money record" not `booking_ledger`; "the sign-up
  page" not `auth/register.blade.php`; "checked in the product" not "verified
  against the code".
- **Send the file itself**, never Markdown and never a file path.
  - **Khadijah prefers XLSX** for anything she maintains — trackers, the decision
    log, checklists (stated 2026-07-31). Build with `openpyxl`: filters on, freeze
    panes, and dropdown validation on Status/Priority so her wording stays
    consistent as she edits.
  - **PDF for anything to be read rather than edited** — Sir Peter's decision
    sheets, reports. Render with the project's dompdf (`Pdf::loadHTML(...)`).
- Keep the technical version in the repo for Ali; send the plain version onward.
  `docs/DECISION_LOG.md` and the PDF are the same content at two reading levels.

**When they already have a file for something, fill in THEIR file — never invent
a parallel one.** Ali caught this on 2026-08-04: *"peter ka jo format hai
checklist wo follow kia hai?"* I had built a nice 5-column status sheet with my
own status words, when their `GigResource_Developer_Change_Checklist_*.xlsx`
already defines 8 columns (User Type · Web Page / Screen · Priority · Issue Type
· Change Needed · **Assigned To** · **Status** · **Notes**), `▸` group rows, and a
locked dropdown: **Not Started / In Progress / Ready for Review / PM Approved**,
with Assigned To = Developer / PM.

**Why:** a parallel file cannot be merged back into their tracker, and my own
status words silently break the PM's workflow. Their vocabulary also encodes
something mine lost — *Ready for Review* is a claim I get to make, *PM Approved*
is Khadijah's to make. Never stamp PM Approved myself.

**How to apply:** copy their file, fill only Status / Assigned To / Notes, leave
structure, widths, freeze panes and validation untouched. Map "blocked on the
client" to Status = Not Started **+ Assigned To = PM** — that is what the column
is for. Extra material goes on a new tab, not by reshaping theirs.

