---

**`~/Downloads/GigResource_Handoff_Package__DEVELOPER__20260808_300AM`** — 216
files, reviewed 2026-08-08. Hash-diffed against the 08-03 pack (177 files):
**46 truly new · 41 changed in place · 7 removed · 0 moved**. Reuse that diff
before reading a future pack — 129 files were unchanged and did not need
re-reading.

**The rulebook jumped R48 → R63.** `GigResource_Rules_Quick_Reference_R1-R63.docx`
replaces the R1-R48 file. The new folder **`17_Marketplace_Eligibility_and_State_Policy`**
(13 files, all dated 08-07) is where the current decisions live.

**The bug this pass found.** R31's 2026-08-05 spreadsheet correction sets
Client Semi = Budget Planner · Smart Checklist · Timeline Builder · **Best
Match** · Message Builder. We had **Review Builder** in the Marketplace slot
instead, read from Khadijah's earlier draft. Fixed 2026-08-08 (`03af332`).

**Why nothing caught it:** both sets are five tools, so every count assertion
passed while the wrong tool shipped. `ToolkitTierTableTest` now pins the Semi
set *and* the Maximum-only set by name. **Never assert a config subset by size
alone** — the count is the one property a swap preserves.

**Built 2026-08-08:** R56 login limit (`f8f297c`) · R62 18+ gate (`1b84714`) ·
R38 same-state matching (`ad17680`) · R61's Fit Score amendments (`1ce11b3`) ·
R53 Client Portfolio Page (`5de58af`) · R52 Messages/Threads merge (`ef12d6a`) ·
R60 Attendee Management (`f96eac8`) · R47 per-state accounts (`0e8c1fe`).

R54 + R55 upload pipeline (`1ba4873`).

**Still locked but not built:** R57 SSN/DOB — needs legal/tax counsel and the
exact trigger threshold, so it is not a coding decision.

**Every upload now goes through `App\Domain\Uploads\UploadPipeline`** with a
purpose from `config/uploads.php`; an unknown purpose throws by design. The
scanner is bound from config and currently reports **not_scanned, never
clean** — do not "simplify" that to a boolean. Verification docs and event
media were on the PUBLIC disk before this; they are private and read through
`UploadedFileController`. The `private` disk itself did not exist in
`config/filesystems.php`, so every chat attachment upload had been throwing.

**R47's real gap was the editable state field**, not the multi-account model —
emails were already unique, phone was already shareable, and R38 already scoped
each account. A professional could flip `profile.state` (free text, 100 chars)
and carry their reviews into a state they were never licensed in. Now fixed
once set; clients deliberately untouched (R38 finding 15 is still open).

**Four R53 sections ship switched off** in `config/client-portfolio.php` —
badges, event history, favourite professionals, working style. Each is built,
not stubbed; one flag each once Peter answers. Same for the R60
professional-access toggle's exact wording and the `/client-portfolio/{id}`
URL, both flagged as recommendations.

**Three traps the 08-08 build hit, worth not repeating:**

`parent::` cannot reach a method that comes from a **trait** used in the same
class — the trait is flattened into that class, so `parent::` misses it and
`Controller::__call` turns it into a runtime `BadMethodCallException`. Every
login 500'd and the tests still passed, because they only read the database and
the counter was written before the response failed. Alias the trait method
(`use T { m as protected baseM; }`) and put `withoutExceptionHandling()` in
`setUp()` for anything auth-shaped.

**Migrations must run on SQLite too.** `UPDATE ... JOIN` is MySQL-only; so are
`CURDATE()` and `HAVING` on a non-aggregate query, both of which were sitting in
`/browse` and are why that page had never had a passing test. Use
`CURRENT_DATE`, `whereHas`, and a portable per-group backfill loop.

**R38 is fail-closed and that is deliberate** — a row with no state matches
nobody. It hid 74 demo events on the dev DB (none published+unassigned, so no
board row was lost); the six demo clients behind them still need states.

Related: [[service-area-gate]] (a different question from R38),
[[pro-category-relation]], [[category-taxonomy-v2]]

**Read before quoting the feed rule:** "related" is **structural** — same Sub
category, different Sub-Sub — *not* a Fit Score threshold. The memo's arithmetic
is the reason: off-category caps at 60 for an established pro and ~40 for a new
one, so any threshold above 40 locks out exactly the professionals the feature
exists for.

