## memory/request-types-esr.md

---
name: request-types-esr
description: "ESR = Emergency/Rush Service Request — its own standalone workflow (parallel to SSR/MSR/Direct Offer), NOT just an MSR link. Currently only a derived label."
metadata: 
  node_type: memory
  type: project
  originSessionId: 85789cbb-33fa-475d-a481-c0a769b18371
  modified: 2026-07-23T12:12:37.749Z
---

Peter's request-type model (clarified 2026-07-14). Four PARALLEL client request workflows, each single- OR multi-service:

- **SSR** = Single Service Request = "Post a Gig / Create a Gig / Post an Event" (one service).
- **MSR** = Multi-Service Request (several services in one gig; pros bid — see [[packages-vs-msr]]).
- **Direct Offer** = client invites a specific pro directly (`ClientDirectOfferController`).
- **ESR** = **Emergency / Rush / Last-Minute Service Request**. Its OWN standalone workflow, parallel to the above, with **single- and multi-service variants** (same pattern as Direct Offer). Keep the label wording "Emergency / Urgent / Last-Minute" — don't reword.

**Peter's rule:** ESR must be a REAL workflow, **not just web-linked to the MSR page**. UI: *"Need help now? Post your request and verified pros can apply right away."* → button **"Post a Rush Request"**. Rush post = urgent flag + short deadline; verified pros can apply/bid immediately.

**Current state (2026-07-23): BUILT.** `ClientEsrController` + `client/esr/create` (routes `client.esr.create` / `client.esr.store`). Publishes a real Event with `source='esr'`, which flows through the normal bids → proposals → award → booking engine.

**Single/multi scope (added 2026-07-23, answering Peter's "could an ESR actually be an SSR and an MSR?"):** yes — two-card chooser at the top of the ESR form, `scope=single|multi`, defaults to multi, deep-linkable via `?scope=single`.

**The distinction Peter was checking, and the thing not to blur:** scope is the *only* thing Direct Offer and ESR share. A Direct Offer is **targeted** — one named pro, `supplier_id` set, `is_published=false`, never hits the board. An ESR is **broadcast** — published, every available pro bids. The ESR rail says this in copy on purpose; don't "unify" the two flows.

Scope is not a column — it's derived from the attached category count, which is literally what single-vs-multi service means. Guard in `store()` rejects `scope=single` with >1 service.

`<x-service-picker>` has a `single` prop for this. Enforce single-select **inside** the component, never from page JS — the picker owns its chips + counters, so an outside handler unchecking boxes leaves them stale. Flip it live by dispatching `svc:single` (`{detail:{on:true}}`) at the picker root; switching multi→single keeps the first pick.

**Pro bidding board (verified 2026-07-23):** `ProfessionalBiddingBoardController::mapEvent()` reads `source === 'esr' ? 'ESR' : (count($cats) >= 2 ? 'MSR' : 'SSR')` — correct. The `urgent` flag also forces true for ESR, because it otherwise came only from "starts within 3 days" and an ESR dated further out lost the flag that is the whole point. Direct Offers never reach the board (query filters `is_published=true`; offers are false).

Left alone: `FindGigsController` still carries a hardcoded representative CATALOG with its own SSR/MSR/ESR labels — demo data, not derived from events.

Also note packages ([[packages-vs-msr]]) are a SEPARATE concept (pro-authored bundles clients browse) — never fold ESR/MSR into packages.


## memory/service-area-gate.md

---
name: service-area-gate
description: "Out-of-area accounts register but can't transact — EnsureServiceArea (fail-closed) + /app/admin/waitlist. Includes two data bugs a column default caused."
metadata:
  type: project
---

Peter's registration rule (2026-07-30) is: everyone registers, eligibility is
revealed afterwards. Built 2026-07-31 (`97f5912`, `9537596`).

**The line is writes, not pages.** An out-of-area account keeps its profile,
its planning tools and a browsable marketplace; it cannot commit, because every
commitment ends in a contract we can't service in that state.
`app/Http/Middleware/EnsureServiceArea.php` is appended to the **web group**,
not hung off route groups, so it **fails closed** — a transactional route added
later is gated the day it's written. Non-GET blocked except an account-scoped
allowlist (profile, password, logout, waitlist opt-in, `ai-tools.` compute);
GET blocked only at the front doors of the request wizards. Admins exempt.

**Two bugs found only by writing the test — both would have shipped as an outage:**
1. `service_area_status` was added with `default('coming_soon')`, which
   backfilled **every existing row** as out-of-area regardless of location —
   all 15 accounts, including the 10 demo pros seeded into the launch states
   for Peter's screenshots. Harmless while nothing read the column; a total
   lockout the moment the gate enforces it. Fixed by a re-runnable backfill
   migration that derives status from the location already on the profile.
2. Even after backfilling, every demo pro was still `coming_soon`: `country` is
   stored as `"United States"` in places while `ServiceArea` compared to `"US"`.
   `ServiceArea::countryCode()` now resolves both.

**Design rule that came out of it:** `service_area_status` is **derived, never
typed** — recomputed in `UserProfile::booted()` on `saving` when country/state
are dirty, because four controllers can write those fields. Move into a launch
state and the same save un-gates you.

Admin side: `/app/admin/waitlist` (`AdminWaitlistController`), read-only by
design — opening a state has legal/payment consequences and shouldn't be a
button. Grouped by state largest-first (the "where do we open next" list) with
opt-in counts; profiles with no state group under "Not given" rather than being
dropped from the total.

Tests: `ServiceAreaGateTest` (11), `AdminWaitlistTest` (4).
Related: [[peter-requirements-backlog]], [[compliance-copy-rules]].


